+
+## Definition
+
+Assume the countable-choice hypothesis carried by the specified smooth non-Abelian gauge-data and principal-connection constructions. Let (G) be a chosen finite-dimensional compact noncommutative Lie group with its global form fixed, (mathfrak g=operatorname{Lie}(G)), and (P\to M) a smooth right principal (G)-bundle over a finite-dimensional Hausdorff, second-countable smooth four-manifold (M). The Euclidean base is either a connected compact oriented Riemannian four-manifold without boundary, or (mathbb R^4) with its standard Euclidean metric and orientation, together with specified smooth compactification data: a framing on the exterior of a ball and a smooth extension of the bundle and connection over the point at infinity to (S^4). In either case the selected connection has finite Euclidean action. The compactification data are part of the field configuration and determine the clutching transition on the overlap; finite action alone is not asserted to imply this extension.
+
+A principal connection is a smooth (mathfrak g)-valued one-form (omega) on (P) satisfying (R_g^*\omega=operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}\omega) and (omega(\xi_P)=\xi) for every (g\in G) and (\xi\in\mathfrak g), where (\xi_P(p)=\left.\frac{d}{dt}\right|_{0}p\exp_G(t\xi)). In a local section its potential is (A), and its curvature is (F_A=dA+\tfrac12[A\wedge A]), using the alternating wedge–Lie bracket, so (\tfrac12[A\wedge A](X,Y)=[A(X),A(Y)]). The Hodge operator is that of the oriented Riemannian metric and obeys \(*^2=1\) on two-forms.
+
+Choose a positive-definite (operatorname{Ad})-invariant inner product (B) on (mathfrak g) and a dimensionless coupling (g_{mathrm{YM}}>0). In natural units (c=\hbar=1), (A_\mu) has mass dimension one, (F_{\mu\nu}) has mass dimension two, (g_{mathrm{YM}}) is dimensionless, and the adopted Euclidean action is the dimensionless model functional
+
+$$S_E[A]=\frac{1}{2g_{mathrm{YM}}^2}\int_M B(F_A\wedge *F_A).$$
+
+For a selected invariant quadratic polynomial \(\mathsf p\) with an explicit integral characteristic-class normalization, let \(\nu(P,A)=\int_M\mathsf p(F_A)\) be the normalized instanton number supplied by the mathematical instanton-number definition. A sector of label \(\nu\) is the set of admitted bundle-connection data with that value; this label is not asserted to classify every connected component or every gauge orbit. For the (SU(2)) specialization, take anti-Hermitian fundamental generators with \(\operatorname{tr}_{\mathbf 2}(T_aT_b)=-\tfrac12\delta_{ab}\), set (B(X,Y)=-2\operatorname{tr}_{\mathbf2}(XY)), and use the convention
+
+$$\nu(P,A)=-\frac{1}{8\pi^2}\int_M\operatorname{tr}_{\mathbf2}(F_A\wedge F_A)=-c_2(P\times_{\mathbf2}\mathbb C^2).$$
+
+The last equality and integrality are conditional on the matching Chern–Weil and integral-class hypotheses; this (SU(2)) coefficient is not transferred to another global form or representation. With this sign, a positive-charge self-dual configuration has \(F_A=*F_A\), and a negative-charge anti-self-dual configuration has \(F_A=-*F_A\). A classical Euclidean saddle means a smooth admissible connection whose first variation of (S_E) vanishes for every smooth compactly supported connection variation preserving the boundary data. An instanton (respectively anti-instanton) saddle is a nonzero-charge saddle satisfying the positive (respectively negative) self-duality equation.
+
+A source observable is a gauge-invariant function (O) on the admissible connection data, where gauge transformations are smooth principal-bundle automorphisms over (operatorname{id}_M). The notation
+
+$$Z_{\mathrm{formal}}(\theta;O)=\sum_{\nu}e^{i\theta\nu}\int_{\mathcal A_{\nu}(P)}\mathcal D A\;O[A]e^{-S_E[A]}$$
+
+is only a formal continuum sector expression. Here \(\theta\) is dimensionless and has period (2\pi) only when \(\nu\) is integral. This notation defines no functional measure, gauge fixing or orbit-volume prescription, determinant, convergent integral, Hilbert-space sector, or continuum quantum field theory. No finite regulator or limit is selected by this definition.
+
+## Definition references
+
+[Tong, §§1.1–1.1.1, complete instanton lecture](https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/tasi/instanton.pdf)
+
+[Vandoren and van Nieuwenhuizen, §§1–2, complete instanton lecture notes](https://arxiv.org/pdf/0802.1862)
