---

## Definition

Assume countable choice $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$, meaning that every countable family of nonempty sets has a choice function, for the specified smooth non-Abelian gauge-data and principal-connection constructions. Let $G$ be a chosen finite-dimensional compact noncommutative Lie group with its global form fixed, let $\mathfrak g=\operatorname{Lie}(G)$, and let $P\to M$ be a smooth right principal $G$-bundle over a finite-dimensional Hausdorff, second-countable smooth four-manifold $M$. Choose a finite-dimensional smooth representation $\rho:G\to\operatorname{GL}(V_\rho)$ when a Chern–Weil charge is used, with associated vector bundle $E_\rho=P\times_\rho V_\rho$. The Euclidean base is either a connected compact oriented Riemannian four-manifold without boundary, or $\mathbb R^4$ with its standard Euclidean metric and orientation, together with specified smooth compactification data: a framing on the exterior of a ball and a smooth extension of the bundle and connection over the point at infinity to $S^4$. The compactification data are part of the field configuration and determine the transition map between the two local trivializations; finite action alone is not asserted to imply this extension.

The model supplies a set $\Sigma$ of admitted bundle sectors, where two bundles are identified when a smooth equivariant diffeomorphism of their total spaces covers $\operatorname{id}_M$; $\Sigma$ may be a singleton.

A principal connection is a smooth $\mathfrak g$-valued one-form $\omega$ on $P$ satisfying $R_g^*\omega=\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}\omega$ and $\omega(\xi_P)=\xi$ for every $g\in G$ and $\xi\in\mathfrak g$, where $\xi_P(p)=\left.\frac{d}{dt}\right|_{0}p\exp_G(t\xi)$. In a local section its potential is $A$, and its curvature is $F_A=dA+\tfrac12[A\wedge A]$, using the alternating wedge–Lie bracket, so $\tfrac12[A\wedge A](X,Y)=[A(X),A(Y)]$. The Hodge operator is that of the oriented Riemannian metric and obeys $*^2=1$ on two-forms.

Choose a positive-definite $\operatorname{Ad}$-invariant inner product $B$ on $\mathfrak g$ and a dimensionless coupling $g_{\mathrm{YM}}>0$. In natural units $c=\hbar=1$, $A_\mu$ has mass dimension one, $F_{\mu\nu}$ has mass dimension two, $g_{\mathrm{YM}}$ is dimensionless, and the adopted Euclidean action is the dimensionless model functional
$$S_E[A]=\frac{1}{2g_{\mathrm{YM}}^2}\int_M B(F_A\wedge *F_A).$$

Admissibility requires the finite-action condition $S_E[A]<\infty$.

For a selected invariant quadratic polynomial $\mathsf p$ with an explicit integral characteristic-class normalization, let $\nu(P,A)=\int_M\mathsf p(F_A)$ be the normalized instanton number of the mathematical instanton-number definition. Let $\mathcal A_\nu(\Sigma)$ be the disjoint union, over admitted bundle sectors in $\Sigma$, of their admissible connections with charge $\nu$. This label is not asserted to classify every connected component or every gauge orbit. For the $SU(2)$ specialization, take anti-Hermitian fundamental generators with $\operatorname{tr}_{\mathbf 2}(T_aT_b)=-\tfrac12\delta_{ab}$, set $B(X,Y)=-2\operatorname{tr}_{\mathbf2}(XY)$, and use the convention
$$\nu(P,A)=-\frac{1}{8\pi^2}\int_M\operatorname{tr}_{\mathbf2}(F_A\wedge F_A)=-c_2(E_{\mathbf2}).$$
Here $E_{\mathbf2}=P\times_{\mathbf2}\mathbb C^2$. The equality with the integral second Chern class is conditional on the matching Chern–Weil and integral-class hypotheses. In the characteristic-form representation branch, full Axiom of Choice is an additional assumption, meaning every family of nonempty sets has a choice function, as required by the cited characteristic-class representation theorem. The representation used for $\mathsf p$ is selected from the supplied unitary representation data with its stated Hermitian form. This $SU(2)$ coefficient is not transferred to another global form or representation.

With this sign, a positive-charge self-dual configuration has $F_A=*F_A$, and a negative-charge anti-self-dual configuration has $F_A=-*F_A$. A classical Euclidean saddle means a smooth admissible connection whose first variation of $S_E$ vanishes for every smooth compactly supported connection variation preserving the boundary data. An instanton (respectively anti-instanton) saddle is a nonzero-charge saddle satisfying the positive (respectively negative) self-duality equation.

A source observable is a gauge-invariant function $O$ on the admissible connection data, where gauge transformations are smooth principal-bundle automorphisms over $\operatorname{id}_M$. The notation
$$Z_{\mathrm{formal}}(\theta;O)=\sum_{\nu}e^{i\theta\nu}\int_{\mathcal A_{\nu}(\Sigma)}\mathcal D A\;O[A]e^{-S_E[A]}$$
is only a formal continuum sector expression over connections, with gauge redundancy left untreated. Here $\theta$ is dimensionless and has period $2\pi$ only when $\nu$ is integral. This notation defines no functional measure, gauge fixing or orbit-volume prescription, determinant, convergent integral, Hilbert-space sector, or continuum quantum field theory. No finite regulator or limit is selected by this definition.

## Definition references

[Tong, §§1.1–1.1.1, complete instanton lecture](https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/tasi/instanton.pdf)

[Vandoren and van Nieuwenhuizen, §§1–2, complete instanton lecture notes](https://arxiv.org/pdf/0802.1862)

[[def-countable-choice|Countable-choice assumption for the supplied connection construction]]

[[def-axiom-of-choice|Full choice assumption for the characteristic-form representation branch]]


