## Definition

Assume countable choice $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$, meaning that every countable family of nonempty sets has a choice function, for the specified smooth non-Abelian gauge-data and principal-connection constructions. Let $G$ be a chosen finite-dimensional compact noncommutative Lie group with its global form fixed, let $\mathfrak g=\operatorname{Lie}(G)$, and let $P\to M$ be a smooth right principal $G$-bundle over a finite-dimensional Hausdorff, second-countable smooth four-manifold $M$. Choose a finite-dimensional smooth representation $\rho:G\to\operatorname{GL}(V_\rho)$ when a Chern–Weil charge is used, with associated vector bundle $E_\rho=P\times_\rho V_\rho$. The Euclidean spacetime $M$ is either a connected compact oriented Riemannian four-manifold without boundary, or standard oriented Euclidean $\mathbb R^4$ with specified smooth compactification data: a framing on the exterior of a ball and a smooth extension of the bundle and connection over the point at infinity to $S^4$. Write $X=M$ and $\bar P=P$, $\bar A=A$ in the compact case; in the flat-space branch write $X=S^4$ and let $\bar P,\bar A$ denote the specified extension, with $P=\bar P|_{\mathbb R^4}$. The compactification data determine the transition map between the local trivializations and are part of the field configuration. Finite action alone is not asserted to imply this extension.

The model supplies a set $\Sigma$ of admitted smooth principal-bundle isomorphism classes over $X$, where two bundles are identified when a smooth equivariant diffeomorphism of their total spaces covers $\operatorname{id}_X$; the selected compactified bundle $\bar P$ represents an element of $\Sigma$, and $\Sigma$ may be a singleton.

A principal connection is a smooth $\mathfrak g$-valued one-form $\omega$ on $P$ satisfying $R_g^*\omega=\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}\omega$ and $\omega(\xi_P)=\xi$ for every $g\in G$ and $\xi\in\mathfrak g$, where $\xi_P(p)=\left.\frac{d}{dt}\right|_{0}p\exp_G(t\xi)$. In a local section its potential is $A$, and its curvature is $F_A=dA+\tfrac12[A\wedge A]$, using the alternating wedge–Lie bracket, so $\tfrac12[A\wedge A](X,Y)=[A(X),A(Y)]$. The Hodge operator is that of the oriented Riemannian metric and obeys $*^2=1$ on two-forms.

Choose a positive-definite $\operatorname{Ad}$-invariant inner product $\kappa$ on $\mathfrak g$ and a dimensionless coupling $g_{\mathrm{YM}}>0$. In natural units $c=\hbar=1$, $A_\mu$ has mass dimension one, $F_{\mu\nu}$ has mass dimension two, $g_{\mathrm{YM}}$ is dimensionless, and the Euclidean action functional specified for this conditional model is
$$S_E[A]=\frac{1}{2g_{\mathrm{YM}}^2}\int_M \kappa(F_A\wedge *F_A).$$

In the flat-space branch, admissibility also requires the finite-action condition $S_E[A]<\infty$ on the $\mathbb R^4$ restriction.

For a selected invariant quadratic polynomial $\mathsf p$ with an explicit integral characteristic-class normalization, let $\nu(\bar P,\bar A)=\int_X\mathsf p(F_{\bar A})$ be the normalized instanton number of the mathematical instanton-number definition. Let $\mathcal A_\nu(\Sigma)$ be the disjoint union, over admitted bundle sectors in $\Sigma$, of their admissible connections with charge $\nu$. This label is not asserted to classify every connected component or every gauge orbit. For the $SU(2)$ specialization, take anti-Hermitian fundamental generators with $\operatorname{tr}_{\mathbf 2}(T_aT_b)=-\tfrac12\delta_{ab}$, set $\kappa(X,Y)=-2\operatorname{tr}_{\mathbf2}(XY)$ and $B_{\mathrm{top}}(X,Y)=-\operatorname{tr}_{\mathbf2}(XY)=\kappa(X,Y)/2$, and use the convention
$$\nu(\bar P,\bar A)=-\frac{1}{8\pi^2}\int_X\operatorname{tr}_{\mathbf2}(F_{\bar A}\wedge F_{\bar A})=-c_2(\bar E_{\mathbf2}).$$
Here $\bar E_{\mathbf2}=\bar P\times_{\mathbf2}\mathbb C^2$ is the associated bundle over $X$. The equality with the integral second Chern class is conditional on the matching Chern–Weil and integral-class hypotheses. In the characteristic-form representation branch, full Axiom of Choice is an additional assumption, meaning every family of nonempty sets has a choice function, as required by the cited characteristic-class representation theorem. The representation used for $\mathsf p$ is selected from the supplied unitary representation data with its stated Hermitian form. This $SU(2)$ coefficient is not transferred to another global form or representation.

With this sign, a positive-charge self-dual configuration has $F_A=*F_A$, and a negative-charge anti-self-dual configuration has $F_A=-*F_A$. A classical Euclidean saddle means a smooth admissible connection whose first variation of $S_E$ vanishes for every smooth compactly supported connection variation preserving the boundary data. An instanton (respectively anti-instanton) saddle is a nonzero-charge saddle satisfying the positive (respectively negative) self-duality equation.

A source observable is a gauge-invariant function $O$ on the admissible connection data, where gauge transformations are smooth principal-bundle automorphisms over $\operatorname{id}_M$. The notation
$$Z_{\mathrm{formal}}(\theta;O)=\sum_{\nu}e^{i\theta\nu}\int_{\mathcal A_{\nu}(\Sigma)}\mathcal D A\;O[A]e^{-S_E[A]}$$
is only a formal continuum sector expression over connections, with gauge redundancy left untreated. Here $\theta$ is dimensionless and has period $2\pi$ only when $\nu$ is integral. This notation defines no functional measure, gauge fixing or orbit-volume prescription, determinant, convergent integral, Hilbert-space sector, or continuum quantum field theory. No finite regulator or limit is selected by this definition.

